ver medalists from 1964 were again favored – " [:a {:href "/athletes/77492"} "Romuald Klim"] " (URS) and " [:a {:href "/athletes/71338"} "Gyula Zsivótzky"] " (HUN). Zsivótzky was best on marks as the world record holder, one set only one month before the Mexico Olympics. But Klim always defeated him head-to-head, having done so nine straight times, including the 1966 European Championships. Zsivótzky opened in the final with 72.26 (237-1) to lead in round one. He improved in rounds two and three and still led, before fouling his fourth throw. Klim then moved ahead with 73.28 (240-5), but Zsivótzky answered on his fifth throw with 73.36 (240-8¼), and that would win the gold medal. Lázár Lovász (HUN) and Japan’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/72611"} "Takeo Sugawara"] " tied for bronze with 69.78 (228-11¼) but Lovász won that medal based on the second longest throw (69.38-69.06)."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Qualifying"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "477"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/71338"} "Gyula Zsivótzky"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/HUN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/HUN.png"}] "HUN"]] [:td {} "72.60 (1)"] [:td {} "73.36 (1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "label label-default"} [:abbrev {:title "Olympic Record"} "OR"]]] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {:class "bib"} "809"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/77492"} "Romuald Klim"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/URS"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/URS.png"}] "URS"]] [:td {} "66.82 (12)"] [:td {} "73.28 (2)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "label la